```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is at the interface layer on router1, not in the BGP configuration.
Router1's eth0 interface (193.10.11.1/24, the eBGP peering link toward router2)
is in a permanently DOWN state. FRR confirms "Link ups: 0, last: (never)" and
"protodown: off", meaning the interface has never been brought up since the lab
started and FRR itself did not administratively shut it — the kernel-level link
was simply never raised (the equivalent of missing an `ip link set eth0 up` in
the startup script). Because the link is down, the 193.10.11.0/24 connected
route never appears in the kernel FIB, so FRR reports "No path to specified
Neighbor" and the BGP session with 193.10.11.2 (router2) stays permanently in
Active state. With no BGP session, router1 never receives router2's advertisement
for 200.1.1.0/24 (pc2's subnet), leaving a black hole for all traffic sourced
from pc1 (195.11.14.x). Router2 is healthy — its eth0 is UP, its routing table
shows both the connected 193.10.11.0/24 and the BGP-learned 195.11.14.0/24 from
the one brief session, and its eth1 serves pc2 normally. The BGP configuration on
both routers is correct (AS numbers, neighbor addresses, and network statements
are all consistent); rolling back the BGP config will not help. The corrective
fix is to bring router1's eth0 up: execute `ip link set eth0 up` on router1. FRR
will immediately install the 193.10.11.0/24 connected route, re-establish the
eBGP session with router2, and install the 200.1.1.0/24 BGP route, restoring
full pc1 ↔ pc2 connectivity.
=== END ===
